  "slug": "graphene-engineering-5eb6",
  "summary": "Five preprints tie stacking, displacement field or Floquet driving to valley polarization, topological bands and superconductivity in graphene multilayers.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is that stacking geometry, gating and external driving set the low-energy band structure and valley character of graphene multilayers. In ABA trilayer graphene, a displacement field inverts band edges, reverses Berry curvature and produces valley splitting. In 17-layer rhombohedral graphene, superconductivity is reported with valley-imbalanced Fermi pockets and is enhanced by in-plane field. In twisted monolayer-N-layer rhombohedral graphene, narrow topological bands arise from hybridization of Dirac cone and surface states, with layer number as a control. Floquet driving of twisted bilayer heterostructures is predicted to yield valley polarization and Chern-number transitions, while a study of magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene finds hidden f-wave TIVC order near nu=-2. The items are all preprints, and the evidence does not establish a single mechanism across these systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
